# Dependency Management

## Purpose

Keep the Maven build minimal, reproducible, compatible with Spring Boot 3.5, and free of speculative technology.

## Scope

Apply to `pom.xml`, Maven plugins, dependency scopes, version alignment, and dependency-related documentation.

## Mandatory Rules

- Use Maven and the existing Spring Boot parent. Do not add Gradle, Node package managers, or a second build system.
- Preserve Java 21 in `<java.version>` and ensure any added library supports Java 21 and Spring Boot 3.5/Jakarta namespaces.
- Prefer Spring Boot dependency management; omit explicit versions for dependencies managed by the Boot BOM.
- Keep explicitly versioned library families aligned. The three JJWT artifacts must use the same version.
- Use correct scopes: PostgreSQL and JJWT implementations at runtime, test libraries at test scope, and Lombok optional/annotation-processor only.
- Before adding a dependency, search the JDK, Spring Boot starters, and current dependencies for the required capability.
- Do not add Redis, Kafka, AWS, MapStruct, Flyway, Liquibase, Testcontainers, OpenAPI, Docker tooling, or tracing libraries merely because roadmap documents mention them.
- If a new technology is explicitly required, add the smallest supported dependency set, configuration, tests, and documentation in one coherent change.
- Never add repositories, mirrors, credentials, or tokens to `pom.xml`.
- Run `mvn test` after dependency changes and inspect `mvn dependency:tree` when resolving version or classpath conflicts.

## Best Practices

- Prefer a Spring starter when it is the supported integration boundary.
- Remove dependencies that are proven unused, but confirm transitive/runtime behavior first.
- Keep plugin configuration explicit only when the default is insufficient.
- Record why a non-obvious direct dependency is necessary in a concise XML comment or documentation.
- Review security and maintenance status before selecting a new library; choose stable releases compatible with the Boot BOM.

## Anti Patterns

- Pinning a Spring-managed artifact to an arbitrary newer version.
- Adding multiple libraries for the same concern.
- Using compile scope for test-only or runtime-only code.
- Adding a dependency to avoid writing a small amount of straightforward Java.
- Suppressing convergence or vulnerability problems without documenting the tradeoff.

## Examples

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Do not add a version because the Spring Boot parent manages it.

## Checklist

- [ ] The dependency is necessary and not already available.
- [ ] Java 21, Spring Boot 3.5, and Jakarta compatibility were checked.
- [ ] Version and scope follow Maven/Boot conventions.
- [ ] No speculative roadmap technology was added.
- [ ] Configuration, tests, and docs accompany the dependency.
- [ ] `mvn test` passes and dependency conflicts were inspected.
